who are the stoic sages this is a great  question and one that definitely  deserves an answer but it's an answer  that I think most people are not going  to be all that happy with once we start  looking into it carefully we see that  this category of sage or wise person  sofos in Greek sapientes in Latin is  going to be a broad one straddling not  just stoic philosophy but we could say  every wisdom tradition intentional way  of living philosophy is a way of life  not just in the Mediterranean  encompassing you know Greek and Romans  or even expanded to the near East but  across the entire world who possesses  wisdom who embodies wisdom who acts and  thinks and chooses and feels in Wise  ways this is a perennial question  and the stoics do in fact have some  answers about this their answers are a  little bit different than many other  people you know there's that proverbial  idea that a sage comes around like a  phoenix once every 500 years they  measure up to this almost unattainable  Summit of human development  the stoics also portray the sages as in  perfect control of their emotions living  completely in accordance with nature  having all of the virtues which includes  not just the cardinal virtues and their  sub-virtues in you know wisdom Justice  Temperance courage but also being some  poticos being a good drinking companion  or being a good party person being  eroticos being a good lover right so  they're they're the perfect human being  they also possess the capacity for  genuine knowledge not the kind of  knowledge that the rest of us have they  have catalyptica Fantasia in Greek right  which is modeled as you know like a fist  closing in on another fist they also  don't just do appropriate actions or  fulfill their duties the things that  they do are entirely good they have the  right motivation unlike the rest of us  rumdoms or Schmucks or as the ancient  Stokes would call us all fools now this  is where we get to a very interesting  peculiarity of the stoic point of view  they often portray the sage or wise  person as if once you cross that  threshold and you're on this side of  wisdom you are a very different kind of  person everybody else is bad everybody  else is foolish everybody else has wrong  ideas everybody else is in some  disharmony with nature the sage alone  actually has it right and you can say  well  how the hell do I make it over there and  this is where a third category very  important for the stoics comes in the  procope toy or proficientes that is  those who are making progress who are  still not sages probably never will be  but are not just foolish people  somewhere in between how do you crush  the threshold into sagehood you got to  work really really hard and you'll  probably not make it but that doesn't  have to dishearten you as Epictetus  himself says I may not become a Socrates  but at least I can become the Epictetus  the best Epictetus that I can be and so  you know this is an important  set of Reflections we should be very  wary of people in the present who talk  about classic stoic figures as if they  were  indeed sages never getting anything  wrong so Epictetus not a sage will tell  you directly that he isn't Marcus  Aurelius great guy not a sage Seneca  definitely not a sage both of them tell  you that that they aren't the sage  mussonius Rufus is not the sage Heracles  is not the sage so who would the stoics  actually consider to have been Sages  there's two legendary people that come  up quite a bit both of whom might seem a  little bit strange to us in the present  one of them is Heracles or Hercules who  is also viewed as a representative  person by the cynics who of course you  know feed into the stoic philosophy  um they're regarded as he's regarded as  a good person who used his strength to  you know undo Injustice and impose  Justice he winds up being deified being  made one of the Olympian gods eventually  so that kind of makes sense and then  there's Odysseus  who you know he's kind of a Wily tricky  guy lies on a lot of occasions and cages  in ruses he is bold he is courageous he  is also considered to be one of the  wiser of the Greeks Mentor perhaps gives  better counsel than  Odysseus but Odysseus is pretty darn  close those are legendary people who  would actually be included among the  potential sages from you know we could  say living history well Seneca says that  Cato was the closest thing that he ever  saw Cato the younger military commander  stoic practitioner so you know he's a  good representative if you don't know  the story of Cato he uh among many other  things fought in the Civil War against  Julius Caesar reluctantly joining the  side of Pompeii and the Senate because  they were the lesser of two evils but  you know commanding his troops as he  ought to and then committing suicide  afterwards so he couldn't be used as a  propaganda tool by the Victorious Caesar  two other people who come up quite  frequently Socrates the great  philosopher understood by the stoics not  primarily through Plato but more through  xenophon and then you know oral  Traditions probably about Socrates  diogenes the cynic who came from sunope  to Athens  acted like kind of a goofy person but  was engaged in trying to uh like  Socrates draw his fellow citizens  towards virtue they're sometimes viewed  by the stoics as being sages with what  we know about them you know sometimes  that might be a little questionable  Epictetus actually has something very  interesting to say about  Socrates diogenes and Zeno the founder  of stoicism he tells us that each of  them did what they ought to do each of  them served the Gods  by doing different things Socrates went  around and asked people questions  pointed questions right uh diogenes went  around and haranged people got in their  face and said the life you're living is  crap you need to act in a different way  Zeno is much more sedate and calm and  taught people and it's it's kind of  intimated that 